Now I understand the setup. The `outline->points` array is allocated by the glyph loader with enough space for the glyph points plus 4 phantom points. The `n_points` passed to `TT_Vary_Apply_Glyph_Deltas` includes the 4 phantom points.

Now, the key question is: where is the WRITE 8 heap-buffer-overflow? Let me think about the `ft_var_readpackedpoints` function. It reads packed points. The `points` array is allocated with `n + 1` slots. But in the loop, `points[i++]` is written. Let me check if `i` can exceed `n`.

In `ft_var_readpackedpoints`:
```c
n = FT_GET_BYTE();
if ( n == 0 )
  return ALL_POINTS;

if ( n & GX_PT_POINTS_ARE_WORDS )
{
  n  &= GX_PT_POINT_RUN_COUNT_MASK;
  n <<= 8;
  n  |= FT_GET_BYTE();
}

if ( n > size )
{
  ...
  return NULL;
}

if ( FT_NEW_ARRAY( points, n + 1 ) )
  return NULL;

*point_cnt = n;

first = 0;
i     = 0;
while ( i < n )
{
  runcnt = FT_GET_BYTE();
  if ( runcnt & GX_PT_POINTS_ARE_WORDS )
  {
    runcnt     &= GX_PT_POINT_RUN_COUNT_MASK;
    first      += FT_GET_USHORT();
    points[i++] = first;

    /* first point not included in run count */
    for ( j = 0; j < runcnt; j++ )
    {
      first      += FT_GET_USHORT();
      points[i++] = first;
      if ( i >= n )
        break;
    }
  }
  ...
}
```

The array is allocated with `n + 1` slots. The loop writes `points[i++]` where `i` can go up to `n` (since the loop condition is `i < n`, and inside the loop `i` can be incremented to `n`). So the maximum index written is `n`, which is within the `n + 1` allocation. So this is safe.

Wait, but there's a subtle issue. In the inner loop, `points[i++] = first;` is executed before the `if ( i >= n ) break;` check. So if `i` was `n-1` at the start of the inner loop iteration, then `points[n-1] = first; i = n;` then `if (i >= n) break;`. So the max index written is `n-1` in the inner loop. But in the outer loop, `points[i++] = first;` where `i < n`, so max index is `n-1`. So the max index written is `n-1`, within the `n+1` allocation. Safe.

